this next story is a political story but  it's also sort of a math story you know  I love a good math story and this math  story has to do with the fact that a lot  of people don't understand percentage  versus percentage points now two people  who do understand the difference please  accept my sincere apology that I have to  do this right now but the amount of  confusion over this over the last 24  hours  has forced my hand and I have to do this  so here's the headline Donald Trump's  approval rating is down 10 points or you  could also say 10 percentage points in  the last month so early in the corona  virus pandemic Donald Trump's approval  actually went up all the way up to 49%  in the gallup tracking poll it is now  down to 39% so Trump's approval went  from 49% to 39 percent that's a decline  of 10 points the problem here is that  some people don't understand the  difference between percent and  percentage points others understand the  concept but are using the wrong term and  just confusing other people so let's  just work through a very quick example  if someone has 50% approval and their  approval goes down by 10% strictly  speaking that would mean 50% 10 percent  of 50 is 5 that means if you have 50%  approval and approval goes down 10% you  go from 50 to 45 okay 10 percent of 50  is 5 so far I think we're clear if you  have an approval of 50% and it drops 10  points also said 10 percentage points  that's a decline of 10 actual points  meaning you went from 50 to 40 you lost  10 points 50 minus 10 is 40 the  confusion comes when we're talking about  percent changes to things already  measured in percent okay so in this case  our real-world numbers our Trump's  approval spiked to 49 and then drop to  39 in a one-month period that's a  10-point decline but it is about a 20%  decline to his approval right if you  lose 10 out of 49 that's a  about 20% and if you're now thoroughly  confused forget everything I've said the  news part of this story is that trumps  approval went from 49 to 39 that's a  very rapid decline in fact I think it's  the largest one-month decline that Trump  has seen at any time during his  presidency now there's good news and  there's bad news the good news is that  clearly Donald Trump's approval is not  immune from his actions in other words  the failed coronavirus response the  totalitarian instincts that came to  light after the George Floyd killing and  the protests that started this has hurt  Donald Trump that's good he's not immune  the bad news is that somehow 4 out of 10  Americans still think he's doing a good  job so this may be a good time to remind  you that when it comes to presidential  approval when you think about how  divided the country is presidential  approval adds up to a hundred but  there's not really a hundred points of  approval in play there's some portion of  people who for ideological reasons will  never approve of the job that the  president does I don't know if that's  like you know 10 percent 15 percent 20  I'm not really sure on the other side  you've got people who will never abandon  Trump no matter what he does that could  be 15 20 25 percent I don't know exactly  what it is right now but the point is  Trump's bottom for approval is not zero  and his top for approval is not 100 even  with George W Bush when things were less  partisan after 9/11 Bush saw a massive  spike in approval to 90 or 88 percent  something like that that was probably  his ceiling and those were the  absolutely most extenuating  circumstances so the point is when  Trump's approval moves from 49 to 39 49  39 it's in that range  it's really a lot more than that because  there the bottom is not zero and the top  is not 100 pick what numbers you think  it is with Trump it's probably something  like I don't think his approval no  matter what he does would go below 25 or  maybe even 28 something like that and I  don't think it would go higher than  probably 80 no matter what he does so in  the context of a range that is more like  28 to 80 a 10-point swing is pretty  significant but  there's a lot of people in this country  who will never abandon him it might be  for their own self esteem they don't  want to admit to themselves that they  made a mistake when they voted Trump in  2016 and the way to at least trick your  brain into thinking you didn't make a  mistake is to vote for him again in 2020  there's this quote incorrectly  attributed to Mark Twain it's still a  good quote just not Mark Twain the quote  is it's easier to fool people than to  convince them that they've been fooled  and along those lines we have to  understand why a lot of 2016 Trump  voters are going to vote for him again  some may abandon him and there's a very  small margin there that Trump can afford  to lose before it becomes almost  impossible to win re-election but  understand it's easier to fool people  than convened to convince them they've  been fooled and it is very relevant in  the times of Trump we'll take a quick  break and be back with much more right  after this make sure you're following me  on Instagram for the latest urban garden  and puppy updates at David Pakman
 

